{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And the bet in במראות.</b> In place of a mem, for it is established in the holy language, as in 'and that which remains of the flesh and of the bread' (Leviticus 8:32), and likewise in the language of Ishmael:" "<b>And the meaning of הצובאות.</b> For the custom of all women was to adorn themselves to see their faces every morning in mirrors of copper or glass, to arrange the ornaments on their heads — these are those mentioned in the book of Isaiah, for the custom of Israel was like the custom of Ishmael until today. Now there were in Israel women who served HaShem, who turned away from the desire of this world, and they gave their mirrors as a donation, for they had no further need to adorn themselves; rather they would come day by day to the entrance of the tent of meeting to pray and to hear the words of the commandments. And this is 'who served [צבאו] at the entrance of the tent of meeting,' for they were many." "And Scripture did not mention the measure of the laver, as it mentioned the measure of the lavers that Solomon made. Rather, it was made from all the mirrors that they brought." "And the ken, which is its stand permanently, is like the measure of the laver, and so is the rule."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:40:50.307339253Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}